<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE PROJECT'S AUTO-FADE IS AUDIBLE AT A CLIP EDGE, AND NOWHERE ELSE.

  Plan 53 P5 (QBX-183), decision D3a (a). A hard clip edge is a step in the
  waveform and a step is a click; before this the only cure was to draw a fade
  by hand at every edge, which nobody does. The auto-fade is a PROJECT property
  (`autoFadeMs`, 3 ms in a new project), applied in the mix at every clip edge
  that has no fade of its own and does not refuse one.

  THE FIXTURE IS TWO BUTT-JOINED CLIPS OF DIFFERENT SOURCES, which is the one
  join the exemption D3b makes can never reach: `test_stereo.wav` over
  [0, 144000) and `test_autosaw.wav` over [144000, 336000). Different content
  objects, so `sautofade::isSeamless` answers no at the very first test and the
  seam is an ordinary pair of edges. `auto_fade_seamless_exempt.qxa` is the
  other half of this pair and uses ONE source read continuously.

  WHY BOTH FIXTURES ARE STATIONARY. Each is constant in amplitude over any
  window this case measures, so the ramp's effect on an RMS is a closed form
  rather than a property of where the window happens to fall.

  THE NUMBERS. A 3 ms fade at 48 kHz is 144 frames, and `twClipFade::gainAt`
  places the ramp ONE FRAME OFF CENTRE at each end — a fade-in's gain is i/144
  for i = 0..143 (so it starts at exactly 0) and a fade-out's is (144-i)/144
  (so it ends at exactly 1/144, not 0). The two RMS factors therefore differ,
  and asserting both pins that definition:

      fade-out:  sqrt( (1/144) * sum_{k=1..144} (k/144)^2 )  =  0.580356
      fade-in:   sqrt( (1/144) * sum_{i=0..143} (i/144)^2 )  =  0.574342

  Applied to the fixtures' own pooled RMS over each window:

      A's tail [143856,144000)   0.402854  ->  0.239580
      A's head [0,144)           0.402488  ->  0.238966
      B's head [144000,+144)     0.236809  ->  0.135987
      B's tail [335856,336000)   0.234485  ->  0.136045

  BUT THE LOAD-BEARING ASSERTIONS HERE ARE THE BYTE COMPARISONS, not the RMS
  bands. `assert-file-identical` takes a frame RANGE (proposal 37 P0a), so this
  case renders the same project twice — once with the auto-fade off, once with
  it at 3 ms — and then says exactly which frames moved and which did not,
  with no tolerance anywhere. Four ranges differ (the two outer edges and the
  two sides of the seam) and everything between them is identical byte for
  byte. That is a far stronger statement than any energy band, and it is what
  makes a wrong fade LENGTH fail: a 288-frame auto-fade would move frames
  inside a range this case requires to be untouched.

  THE PROJECT STARTS WITH NO AUTO-FADE, deliberately. `setup project="new"`
  goes through `SActionRunner`, which does NOT seed — the 3 ms seed of D3a (a)
  lives on the real fileNew path (`sautofade::seedInto`), so a scripted project
  reads the property's own default of 0 and every case in this suite that
  predates plan 53 renders exactly as it always did. That is the whole safety
  property of D3a, and `auto_fade_seed.qxa` is where the seed's own placement
  is asserted from the other side.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="auto_fade_seam">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- 144000 frames is 1.5 bars at 120 BPM, which is not on the grid. -->
    <snap-to-grid-disable/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_stereo.wav"  timePos="0"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="0" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="144000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,0" startTime="0"      duration="144000"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="0,1" startTime="144000" duration="192000"/>

    <!-- BEFORE: no auto-fade. The project has never had the property set. -->
    <render filename="af_off.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_off.wav" startFrame="143856"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.4008" maxRms="0.4049"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_off.wav" startFrame="144000"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.2356" maxRms="0.2380"/>

    <!-- 3 ms. A project property, so this is a `set-property` and not an
         undoable verb (it is the same route the Options page takes). -->
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[3.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_on.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>

    <!-- THE SEAM FADED, on BOTH sides, by the closed forms above. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_on.wav" startFrame="143856"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.2384" maxRms="0.2408"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_on.wav" startFrame="144000"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.1353" maxRms="0.1367"/>
    <!-- ...and so did the two OUTER edges: the project's start and its end are
         clip edges like any other, which is the point of an AUTO fade. -->
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_on.wav" startFrame="0"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.2378" maxRms="0.2402"/>
    <assert-audio-energy filename="af_on.wav" startFrame="335856"
                         frameCount="144" minRms="0.1353" maxRms="0.1368"/>

    <!-- AND NOW THE EXACT STATEMENT, with no tolerance in it. Everything
         between the four fades is IDENTICAL byte for byte. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="144" frameCount="143712"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="144144" frameCount="191712"/>

    <!-- ...and exactly the four fade windows DIFFER. `expectReject` on
         assert-file-identical is how this suite says "these must not be the
         same" (file_identical_gate.qxa). Without these four the two
         assertions above would also pass with the feature deleted. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="0" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="143856" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="144000" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_on.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="335856" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>

    <!-- CHANGING THE PROPERTY IS AUDIBLE ON ITS OWN. Nothing else notices an
         auto-fade change — no clip changed and no arrangement changed, and
         the lengths live in the mix's per-clip entries — so
         `sautofade::reapplyAll` is wired to SProject::propertyChanged, exactly
         as the metronome property is wired to a plan rebuild. Setting the
         property back to 0 must return the ORIGINAL file, byte for byte,
         WITHOUT any other edit in between. -->
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[0.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_back.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_back.wav" expected="af_off.wav"/>

    <!-- A LONGER AUTO-FADE REACHES FURTHER, which is what makes the LENGTH and
         not merely the presence of a fade gated: 6 ms = 288 frames, so the
         window [143712,143856) that af_on left untouched now moves. -->
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[6.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_6ms.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="7.0"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_6ms.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="143712" frameCount="144"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_6ms.wav" expected="af_on.wav"
                           startFrame="143712" frameCount="288"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <!-- ...and still nothing outside IT: [143424,143712) is untouched. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_6ms.wav" expected="af_off.wav"
                           startFrame="143424" frameCount="288"/>

    <!-- AND THE CLAMP: min( autoFade, clipLen / 2 ). A clip shorter than two
         auto-fades gets half its own length at each edge instead, so it still
         reaches unity in the middle rather than becoming a triangle.

         A SECOND LANE, so none of the arithmetic above moves, and the clip
         sits at 400000 — PAST the end of lane 0's material at 336000 — so the
         window measured below carries that clip and nothing else. A first
         draft put it at 0 and the comparison read lane 0's own head fade
         instead, which differs at 3 ms and 6 ms exactly as it should: the
         measurement was of the wrong lane, not of a broken clamp.

         One 200-frame clip, where 3 ms (144 frames) and 6 ms (288) BOTH clamp
         to 100. That equality is the clamp's own signature — past clipLen/2
         the length stops growing — and it is a byte comparison, not a band. -->
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackIndex="1" filePath="../test_autosaw.wav" timePos="400000"/>
    <resize-clip clip="1,0" startTime="400000" startOffset="0" duration="200"
                 stretch="1.0"/>
    <assert-clip-window clip="1,0" startTime="400000" duration="200"/>

    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[0.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_tiny0.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="9.0"/>
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[3.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_tiny3.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="9.0"/>
    <set-property key="autoFadeMs" value="[6.0]"/>
    <render filename="af_tiny6.wav" format="wav" quality="10" durationSec="9.0"/>

    <!-- The tiny clip DID fade... -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_tiny3.wav" expected="af_tiny0.wav"
                           startFrame="400000" frameCount="200"
                           expectReject="true"/>
    <!-- ...and 6 ms fades it IDENTICALLY, because both clamp to 100 frames.
         Without the clamp, 288 frames of ramp in a 200-frame clip would be a
         different shape from 144 and these two files would differ. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_tiny6.wav" expected="af_tiny3.wav"
                           startFrame="400000" frameCount="200"/>
    <!-- The LONG lane, on the other hand, really is different at 6 ms — so
         the equality above is the clamp and not the property being ignored. -->
    <assert-file-identical actual="af_tiny6.wav" expected="af_tiny3.wav"
                           startFrame="143712" frameCount="288"
                           expectReject="true"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="2"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
